course: history
lesson: 56
title: China: the Ming and Zheng He's voyages
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Ming dynasty, from 1368 to 1644, moved the capital to Beijing and built the Forbidden City, a palace of red walls and golden roofs. Mei says it has 9,999 rooms because only Heaven may have 10,000: a lovely legend, but surveys count about 8,700.
explain: Admiral Zheng He sailed seven great voyages with hundreds of ships to India, Arabia and East Africa, long before Columbus. Picture Columbus's ships parked next to a Ming treasure ship: like rowing boats beside a ferry.
explain: Mei once read that China discovered America in 1421. The teacher says no, and he is right: there is no evidence. The true story already has a giraffe from Africa that the emperor's court mistook for a lucky magical beast.
explain: The Ming also rebuilt the famous brick-and-stone parts of the Great Wall and traded silk and porcelain for silver from the Americas; in the end famine and rebellion inside weakened it before the Manchus came.
fact: The Ming dynasty, 1368–1644, moved the capital to Beijing, where the Forbidden City was built from 1406 to 1420 by many thousands of workers.
fact: The legend says the Forbidden City has 9,999 rooms because only Heaven can have 10,000; surveys count about 8,700.
fact: Admiral Zheng He led seven great voyages from 1405 to 1433 to Southeast Asia, India, Arabia and East Africa, with hundreds of ships and about 27,000 men.
fact: The biggest treasure ships are said to have been about 120 m long; many historians think they were smaller, but they were still much bigger than Columbus's ships.
fact: A 2002 popular book claimed Zheng He's fleets reached America in 1421; historians reject this because there is no evidence in Chinese records, maps or archaeology.
fact: In 1414 a giraffe from Malindi, in today's Kenya, was brought to the Ming court, where people took it for a qilin, a lucky mythical beast, and painted it.
fact: After 1433 the voyages stopped: they were very expensive, court officials opposed them, and the Ming focused on defending the north against the Mongols.
fact: The Ming rebuilt and greatly extended walls in brick and stone, including the famous sections at Badaling and Mutianyu, mostly in the 15th and 16th centuries.
fact: The Italian Jesuit Matteo Ricci, Chinese name Li Madou, lived in China from 1582 to 1610, mastered Chinese, made a world map in Chinese and was buried in Beijing with the emperor's permission.
fact: Rare Ming blue-and-white porcelain can sell for millions at auction, but much Ming porcelain was exported in huge numbers and ordinary pieces are not so valuable.
fact: In the 16th and 17th centuries Spanish silver from the Americas flowed into Ming China through Manila to buy silk and porcelain.
fact: Famine, rebellion and money troubles weakened the Ming; the rebel Li Zicheng took Beijing in 1644, and then the Manchus entered and founded the Qing.
exercise: When did the Ming dynasty rule? | 1368–1644 | 960–1279 | 618–907 | 1644–1912
exercise: How many years did it take to build the Forbidden City? | About fourteen years | Only one single year | One month | About two hundred years
exercise: About how many rooms do surveys count in the Forbidden City? | About 8,700 | Exactly 9,999 | Exactly 10,000 | About 100
exercise: Why would a legend choose 9,999 rooms? | Only Heaven could have 10,000 | The builders ran out of bricks | 9,999 was the emperor's age | It is the number of dragons
exercise: How many great voyages did Zheng He lead? | Seven | Only one | Seventy | Just two
exercise: Which lands did Zheng He's fleets reach? | Southeast Asia, India, Arabia and East Africa | America, Australia and the islands of the Pacific Ocean | Antarctica | Only Japan
exercise: How did the treasure ships compare with Columbus's ships? | They were much bigger | They were much smaller | They were exactly the same | They were made of paper
exercise: Why do historians reject the claim that China discovered America in 1421? | There is no evidence in records, maps or archaeology | The ships could not float | America did not exist yet | Zheng He was afraid of the open sea and never left the coast of China
exercise: Where did the emperor's giraffe come from? | Malindi in today's Kenya | The rainforests of Brazil | The jungles of southern India | The deserts of central Australia
exercise: What did the Ming court take the giraffe for? | A qilin, a lucky mythical beast | A dragon | A very tall horse sent from Arabia as a gift | A Mongol spy
exercise: Why did the voyages stop after 1433? | They were costly, officials opposed them and the north needed defending | China had already found every land there was, so there was nowhere left to sail | The sea dried up | Zheng He became emperor
exercise: Which famous Wall sections did the Ming build? | Badaling and Mutianyu | Hadrian's Wall in Britain | The Berlin Wall | The Wailing Wall
exercise: What did Matteo Ricci make for Chinese readers? | A world map in Chinese | The very first magnetic compass | A blue-and-white Ming vase | The first paper money of China
exercise: What was Matteo Ricci's Chinese name? | Li Madou | Li Bai the poet | Admiral Zheng He | Bi Sheng the printer
exercise: Through which port did American silver reach China? | Manila | London in England | Venice in Italy | Lisbon in Portugal
exercise: Who took Beijing first in 1644? | The rebel Li Zicheng | The British navy | The Mongols of the north | Zheng He
test: Mei says the Forbidden City was built in one year. What did the teacher have right? | It took from 1406 to 1420 | One year is right | It took one week | It was never finished
test: A tour guide repeats «9,999 rooms». What would Mei, who read the survey, say? | A lovely legend; surveys count about 8,700 | It is exactly true, every room has been counted | It has 10,000 and one | It has no rooms
test: omg mei says china found america in 1421!! true? | No evidence; Zheng He's farthest voyages reached East Africa | Yes, proven by old Chinese maps that show the coast of California | Yes, he founded New York | No, he went to the Moon
test: What evidence would historians need to prove a Ming voyage to America? | Records, maps or remains found by archaeologists | A popular book | A film | A strong feeling that it must be true, shared by many readers
test: Mei: «Africa sent a dragon to China!» What really arrived? | A giraffe the court took for a qilin | A real dragon | A lion | An elephant
test: The teacher says China stopped exploring because it «closed itself». Too simple? | Yes; cost, opposition at court and the northern defence mattered | No, that is the whole story, and nothing else at all needs to be said | China never explored | The ships sank
test: If you found a Ming bowl in Nan's attic, would it be worth millions? | Probably not; only rare pieces are, many were made | Yes, every Ming piece is priceless | It would be worthless whatever | Only if it is broken
test: Why did Spanish silver travel all the way to China? | To buy silk and porcelain | To build the Wall | To pay Zheng He | To make coins for Rome
test: Mei says the Ming fell only because of the Manchus. What happened first? | Famine, rebellion and money troubles; Li Zicheng took Beijing | The Mongols returned | The British invaded with gunboats and soldiers from India | Nothing; the Ming are still ruling
test: How could a European like Ricci be buried in Beijing with honour? | He learned Chinese well and was greatly respected | He conquered China | He was the emperor's son | He was a Ming admiral
test: Mei says the Ming built the whole Wall from scratch. What is fairer? | They built the famous brick and stone parts; earlier walls existed | Exactly right | The Ming built no walls | The Qin built every part, and the Ming only painted it and put up flags
exam: Identify the period during which the voyages commanded by Zheng He were undertaken. | From 1405 to 1433 | From 221 to 206 BC | From 1839 to 1842 | From 1492 to 1504
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the assertion that a Chinese fleet reached America in 1421. | It is rejected by historians for want of any evidence | It is confirmed by Ming charts preserved in Beijing | It is attested by Columbus himself | It is established by the giraffe of 1414
exam: Ascertain the principal reasons for the cessation of the Ming maritime expeditions after 1433. | Their expense, opposition at court and the priority of northern defence | The exhaustion of all lands to be discovered | The destruction of the entire fleet by the Portuguese navy in the Indian Ocean | The death of every sailor at sea
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the animal presented to the Ming court in 1414? | A giraffe from Malindi, taken to be the auspicious qilin | A dragon captured in the Yellow River | A panda sent from Tibet | An elephant from Rome
